 "interpretationRules": {
  "encodedRules": [],
  "sourceGuidance": [
   {
    "title": "TAFS-R Scoring & Interpretation",
    "text": "Raw scores and percentiles for the three subscales are given as output, with higher scores indicating a stronger tendency toward thought-action-fusion like cognitions.\n\nFor each subscale, percentiles based on a student and clinical sample are computed using Safron et al., (1996) original validation samples. A percentile of 50 for the OCD clinical sample represents the average amount of TAF thoughts that a person with OCD experiences, while a percentile of 50 on the student sample indicates the average TAF thoughts that a relatively healthy individual experiences. Percentiles are not computed for the total score.\n\nThe three subscales are as follows:\n1. TAF-Moral (items: 1, 3, 4, 6, 8, 10, 11, 13, 15, 17, 18, 19): The belief that having an unacceptable thought is the moral equivalent of carrying out the unacceptable or disturbing action.\n2. TAF-Likelihood-Other (items: 2, 5, 7, 9): The belief that thinking an unacceptable thought about another person increases the likelihood of that thing happening.\n3. TAF-Likelihood-Self (items: 12, 14, 16): The belief that thinking an unacceptable thought about ourselves makes it more likely to occur.\n\nOn first administration, the subscale percentiles are presented compared to the clinical OCD sample with a shaded colour in the background for the higher percentiles (indicating more Thought-Action-Fusion). On subsequent administrations the subscale percentiles are presented over time to indicate change as a result of treatment.\n",
    "source_url": null
   }
  ],
  "reportScoringGuidance": [
   {
    "documentSha256": "bb4360f1a59424c915a3ec96e9682a6891f9aba1f600d712517c1e5b6368838e",
